---
title: The Boundary
summary: Every island is a serialization boundary. import.meta.og.$ is the one mark that carries anything across it, functions included, into context or props. Stores cross automatically, and refusals fail at your line rather than inside a serializer.
related:
  - data-state/state-context
  - macros/wire
  - regions/client-islands
---

Each island is its own hydration root, so every value that travels from a server-rendered host into an island, whether a prop or a context value, crosses a **serialization boundary**. Plain data crosses without any work on your part, but every other value needs a way to travel. ogygia's rule for this fits in one sentence:

> **Mark the boundary with `import.meta.og.$()`. Everything that crosses is either data, or
> something that knows how to rebuild itself on the other side.**

## `import.meta.og.$`: the one mark

You wrap a value in `og.$` at the exact place where it goes to a boundary, and what the mark does depends on what you give it.

### A function: it crosses _alive_

```svelte
<script>
	import { setContext } from 'ogygia';

	const tax = 0.19; // a local — captured by VALUE

	setContext('fmt', import.meta.og.$((n) => `€${(n * (1 + tax)).toFixed(2)}`));
</script>
```

```svelte
<!-- inside any island, plain svelte: -->
<script>
	import { getContext } from 'svelte';
	const fmt = getContext('fmt'); // a REAL, callable function
</script>
<p>{fmt(100)}</p> <!-- €119.00 — synchronous, no eval in production -->
```

The compiler hoists the function and turns each captured local into a bound value that crosses as data, and the island binds it again on the other side. The function itself is **never serialized**: its code ships as a build artifact, and its captures ship like any prop. Because captures follow the same rules as props, they have to be serializable. A captured store crosses live and stays shared, as described [below](#stores-automatic-shared-live).

Two mistakes are build errors, reported at your file and line:

- a capture from a **server-only module** (`$env/static/private`, `$app/server`,
  `*.server.ts`), because the value would ship into client HTML;
- a mark on something that can never cross (a DOM node, a bare function inside an object), so the
  error fires at the mark instead of later inside a serializer.

### Anything else: an explicit, checked crossing

```svelte
<Cart
	items={import.meta.og.$(cartStore)}
	onCheckout={import.meta.og.$((id) => `/checkout/${id}`)}
/>
```

For a value that is not a function, `og.$` is a **boundary assertion**: the value is classified on that line. A store passes and will be wired automatically, and plain data passes too. A live DOM node, or a class instance without a codec, **throws on that line**, with the path inside the value that caused it. The value itself is returned untouched, so there is no wrapper to unlearn.

You never _need_ `og.$` for values that cross on their own (see the table below), but the mark is the recommended style, because the next reader can see exactly where the page splits and mistakes surface at the mark instead of at serialization time.

## What crosses, and how

| You pass                                           | How it travels                                                                            | Needs a mark?                                                                            |
| -------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| plain data, `Map`/`Set`/`Date`                     | serialized (devalue)                                                                      | no                                                                                       |
| a **store** (`subscribe`-shaped)                   | value crosses; islands **join to ONE live instance**: `set` in island A repaints island B | no, automatic                                                                            |
| a **factory store** (custom methods)               | value crosses; methods **rebuilt from the factory** on the other side                     | usually no: provable factories auto-brand; assert with `og.store` (below) in other cases |
| a **class instance**                               | through its own codec: [`import.meta.og.wire`](/docs/macros/wire)                         | once, on the class                                                                       |
| a **function**                                     | hoisted code plus bound captures (`og.$`)                                                 | yes, `og.$`                                                                              |
| a **snippet**                                      | freezes to HTML, or crosses live (`{#snippet}`)                                           | no                                                                                       |
| a component / UI                                   | it is a [region](/docs/regions/held-regions), deliberately a different noun               | `region()` / `<Region>`                                                                  |
| a DOM node, a bare callback in an object, a secret | **refused**, with the path and a fix                                                      | —                                                                                        |

## Stores: automatic, shared, live

```svelte
<script>
	import { setContext } from 'ogygia';
	import { writable } from 'svelte/store';
	setContext('theme', writable('dark'));
</script>
```

Every island that reads `getContext('theme')` gets **the same live store**, not a copy. The current value crosses at render time and the identity is joined again on the client, so writes move between islands through ordinary Svelte reactivity. The same store provided under two keys stays one instance.

### Factory stores

A store with custom methods needs its **factory** on the other side, because methods are code and are never serialized. Factories that the compiler can prove are branded automatically:

```ts
// auto-branded: the body provably returns a store
export const createCart = (seed = []) => {
	const { subscribe, set, update } = writable(seed);
	return { subscribe, set, update, add: (item) => update((x) => [...x, item]) };
};
```

For a factory it cannot prove, such as one that returns a helper's result, assert it yourself:

```ts
export const createCart = import.meta.og.store((seed = []) => makeStore(seed));
```

The **factory's first parameter is the seed**, which is the value that crosses. If a branded store reaches an island whose bundle never loaded the factory, it degrades to a plain writable that keeps the value but lacks the methods, and a console warning names the one import that fixes it. It never crashes.

Two store shapes need care. A **`derived`** store crosses as a frozen seed, because the derivation itself cannot travel, so derive it again on the client or share its sources. Store **values** follow the same rules as props, so a callback inside a store's value is still a bare function.

## Keeping things home: `{ islands: false }`

Not everything has to cross. Some context is only read by the server-rendered host, such as a bag of element refs, a host-only callback or a very large config object, and one option keeps it native:

```js
setContext('scrollRefs', refs, { islands: false });
```

Same-tree `getContext` works as always, and nothing is serialized. In dev, ogygia warns when the island context payload grows past 32kB and names the heaviest keys, and this option is usually the fix.

## Policy and errors

The boundary refuses keys that look like secrets (`token`, `apiKey`, …), so credentials don't end up in client HTML. You can adjust this once, on the server:

```ts
// hooks.server.ts
import { configure_boundary } from 'ogygia/internal';

configure_boundary({
	allow: ['tokenColor'],   // a false positive the sniff should let through
	deny: ['sessionBlob']    // a semantic secret the sniff can't see
});
```

Everything the boundary declines comes with an **explanation**: a dropped context key logs the key, the dot-path to the leaf that caused it and what to do instead. The aim is that nothing crosses, or fails to cross, silently.
